   "text": "A home Wi-Fi network carries a video stream from the internet to a laptop through a series of steps involving the router, access points, radio channels, and packets. When you start streaming a video on your laptop, the request is sent to the internet, and the video data is broken down into small packets. These packets travel through the internet to your home network.\n\nThe router, which is the central hub of your home network, receives these packets. The router then forwards them to the access point, which is often the same device as the router or a separate device connected to the router. The access point, which acts as a bridge between the wired network and the wireless network, sends the packets to your laptop over the air using radio waves.\n\nThe radio channels used by Wi-Fi operate in a shared spectrum, meaning multiple devices can transmit and receive data simultaneously. However, this shared nature can lead to interference and conflicts. When two devices, such as your laptop and another device like a smart speaker, transmit at the same time, they can interfere with each other. This is known as a collision, and it can cause data to be lost or corrupted.\n\nTo manage this, Wi-Fi networks use a protocol called Carrier Sense Multiple Access with Collision Avoidance (CSMA/CA).",
